     3	# NOT generated by CI. Written by `scripts/install-npm.sh` when a maintainer
     4	# runs it locally; no workflow invokes that script and no workflow appends
     5	# here on tag push. The entries below therefore describe the last LOCAL
     6	# build, not the published package, and this file is absent from the `files:`
     7	# array in npm/package.json — it does not travel inside the tarball. See
     8	# npm/INTEGRITY.md §SHA-256 tarball manifest (deferred control).
     9	# PLAN-025 Batch I F-comp-002: file reset to exactly 1 line (the tarball of
    10	# the local build at that time) — previously accumulated 51 stale entries
    11	# across rebuilds, which confused adopter integrity checks.
    12	#
    13	# Regeneration — run the writer that actually produces these lines:
    14	#
    15	#   bash scripts/install-npm.sh
    16	#
    17	# It builds the tarball, hashes it with `sha256sum` (or `shasum -a 256`, or
    18	# python hashlib as a last resort), and replaces the line for that filename
    19	# in place. Manual equivalent, when you want the digest without re-staging:
    20	#
    21	#   cd npm && TARBALL=$(npm pack --json | python3 -c \
    22	#     'import json,sys; print(json.load(sys.stdin)[0]["filename"])') && \
    23	#     shasum -a 256 "$TARBALL"      # GNU: sha256sum "$TARBALL"
    24	#
    25	# The previously documented `npm pack --dry-run --json | jq -r '.[0].shasum'`
    26	# CANNOT regenerate this file and is not a substitute: npm's `.shasum` is the
    27	# legacy SHA-1 of the tarball, and it carries no filename, so it produces
    28	# neither the algorithm nor the format of the lines below. `--dry-run` also
    29	# writes no tarball to hash.
    30	#
    31	# Each line below maps: <sha256>  <tarball-name>
    32	# Appended ONLY by a local `scripts/install-npm.sh` run.
    33	#
